  "name": "CERULOPLASMIN BY IMMUNOTURBIDMETRY",
  "code": "1055",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"133\" data-end=\"278\"><strong data-start=\"133\" data-end=\"150\">Ceruloplasmin</strong> is a copper-carrying protein produced by the liver. This test measures ceruloplasmin levels in the blood and is mainly used to:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"280\" data-end=\"535\">\n<li data-start=\"280\" data-end=\"352\">\n<p data-start=\"282\" data-end=\"352\"><strong data-start=\"282\" data-end=\"311\">Diagnose Wilson&rsquo;s disease</strong>, a genetic disorder of copper metabolism</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"353\" data-end=\"397\">\n<p data-start=\"355\" data-end=\"397\">Evaluate <strong data-start=\"364\" data-end=\"397\">copper deficiency or overload</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"398\" data-end=\"453\">\n<p data-start=\"400\" data-end=\"453\">Assess <strong data-start=\"407\" data-end=\"425\">liver function</strong> and <strong data-start=\"430\" data-end=\"453\">inflammatory states</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"454\" data-end=\"535\">\n<p data-start=\"456\" data-end=\"535\">Help investigate <strong data-start=\"473\" data-end=\"498\">neurological symptoms</strong> linked to abnormal copper metabolism</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 data-start=\"542\" data-end=\"592\"><span style=\"font-size: 12px;\">Elevated Levels &ndash; Clinical Implications</span></h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th><strong>Condition</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Clinical Significance</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Acute or chronic inflammation</strong></td>\n<td>Ceruloplasmin is an acute-phase reactant and rises in infections, autoimmune conditions, etc.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Pregnancy</strong></td>\n<td>Levels naturally increase during pregnancy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Estrogen use (oral contraceptives or HRT)</strong></td>\n<td>Estrogens stimulate ceruloplasmin synthesis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Liver diseases</strong> (hepatitis, cirrhosis)</td>\n<td>Mild to moderate elevation may occur, especially in early disease</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Lymphoma or certain cancers</strong></td>\n<td>May cause elevated levels due to increased synthesis or inflammation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>&nbsp;</p>",
